/*! \page page_val AnCH validation library documentation
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section val_intro_seq Introduction
 *
 * AnCH validation library defines fields constraints independently of formats, and validates described objects
 * ( \c anch::Mapping , anch-utils) without serializing them. anch-json checks the constraints when it reads and writes
 * values ; other formats (CSV, XML) and schema
 * generators (JSON Schema, XSD) use the same constraints (see \c anch-val.todo ).
 *
 * \section val_install_sec Installation
 *
 * CMake target \c anch::val (built with anch-json, which depends on it). \c uuid , \c date-time and \c date
 * formats are built with \c ANCH_UUID and \c ANCH_DATE (anch-uuid and anch-date are optional dependencies):
 * \code{.cmake}
find_package(Anch REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my-app PRIVATE anch::val)
\endcode
 * See \ref install_sec "installation".
 *
 * \section val_constraints_sec Constraints
 *
 * Constraints are checked at compile time against the value type: \c minLength on an integer does not compile.
 * \c null and absent values are not checked ( \ref anch::val::required is a field constraint, checked by formats).
 *
 * | Constraint | Field type | JSON Schema keyword |
 * |------------|------------|---------------------|
 * | \c anch::val::minLength(n) , \c anch::val::maxLength(n) | string (length in Unicode code points) | \c minLength , \c maxLength |
 * | \c anch::val::pattern(regex) | string (searched, not anchored) | \c pattern |
 * | \c anch::val::format(name) | string (registered formats only) | \c format |
 * | \c anch::val::minimum(v) , \c anch::val::maximum(v) , \c anch::val::exclusiveMinimum(v) , \c anch::val::exclusiveMaximum(v) | number | \c minimum , \c maximum , \c exclusiveMinimum , \c exclusiveMaximum |
 * | \c anch::val::multipleOf(v) | number (exact for integers, relative tolerance for floating point numbers) | \c multipleOf |
 * | \c anch::val::minItems(n) , \c anch::val::maxItems(n) , \c anch::val::uniqueItems | collection | \c minItems , \c maxItems , \c uniqueItems |
 * | \c anch::val::minProperties(n) , \c anch::val::maxProperties(n) | map | \c minProperties , \c maxProperties |
 * | \c anch::val::allowed({...}) | any comparable type | \c enum |
 * | \c anch::val::items(constraints...) | collection or map: constraints are checked on every item | \c items , \c additionalProperties |
 * | \c anch::val::custom<T>(predicate, message) | \c T | |
 *
 * Patterns use \c std::regex (ECMAScript grammar, on bytes: no UTF-8 character classes) by default:
 * \ref anch::val::setPatternCompiler plugs another engine. Formats are checked by validators registered with
 * \ref anch::val::registerFormat ( \c email , \c hostname , \c ipv4 , \c ipv6 , \c uri , \c byte , and \c uuid ,
 * \c date-time , \c date with \c ANCH_UUID / \c ANCH_DATE are built-in) ; unknown formats are not checked.\n
 * Integers bounds are compared without conversion (an \c uint64_t field accepts \c minimum(-1) ).
 *
 * \section val_xsd_sec XSD facets
 *
 * | XSD facet | Constraint |
 * |-----------|------------|
 * | \c length , \c minLength , \c maxLength | \c anch::val::length(n) , \c anch::val::minLength(n) , \c anch::val::maxLength(n) (strings: code points ; \c length also counts list items) |
 * | \c pattern | \c anch::val::xsdPattern(regex) (matches the whole value) |
 * | \c enumeration | \c anch::val::allowed({...}) |
 * | \c minInclusive , \c maxInclusive , \c minExclusive , \c maxExclusive | \c anch::val::minimum(v) , \c anch::val::maximum(v) , \c anch::val::exclusiveMinimum(v) , \c anch::val::exclusiveMaximum(v) |
 * | \c totalDigits , \c fractionDigits | \c anch::val::totalDigits(n) , \c anch::val::fractionDigits(n) (numbers and decimal strings) |
 * | \c minOccurs , \c maxOccurs | \c anch::val::required , \c anch::val::minItems(n) , \c anch::val::maxItems(n) |
 *
 * \c whiteSpace is a normalization, applied by formats. XSD patterns have their own compiler
 * ( \ref anch::val::setPatternCompiler(anch::val::PatternSyntax, anch::val::PatternCompiler) "setPatternCompiler"):
 * the default one translates them to \c std::regex when the translation keeps their meaning ( \c ^ and \c $ are
 * ordinary characters, \c \\d is ASCII digits) and refuses Unicode properties ( \c \\p{..} , \c \\i , \c \\c ,
 * \c \\w ) and character class subtractions:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-val/examples/val.cpp xsd
 *
 * A constraint is a value with a \c check(value, message) function ; its parameters are public, for schema
 * generators:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-val/examples/val.cpp check
 *
 * Usage with anch-json:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-json/examples/constraints.cpp example
 *
 * \section val_validate_sec Validation
 *
 * \ref anch::val::validate checks a value without serializing it: constraints of the fields of described objects
 * and of their nested values (objects, collections items, maps values, \c std::optional and pointers values). It
 * does not stop on the first violation: every violation is returned with its path ( \ref anch::val::Path "Path":
 * field names, map keys and items indexes, formatted as a JSON Pointer by \c pointer() ), in fields registration
 * order. Fields registered with a setter only are not checked, and required fields are not checked (an
 * object always has its fields). \ref anch::val::isValid returns \c true when there is no violation.\n
 * Values are \ref anch::val::Validatable "validatable" when they are scalars ( \ref anch::val::IsScalar : numbers,
 * enumerations, strings, UUID, dates, ... ; it can be specialized for other value types), described objects, or
 * \c std::optional , pointers, collections and maps of them: a class which is neither described nor a scalar is
 * refused at compile time, as its description is probably not visible (see \c anch::Mapping ).
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-val/examples/val.cpp mapping
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-val/examples/val.cpp validate
 *
 * \section val_context_sec Contexts
 *
 * Constraints are checked in a context ( \ref anch::val::CheckContext ): it enables or disables checks and reports
 * violations. \ref anch::val::ValidationContext collects every violation with its path:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-val/examples/val.cpp validation
 *
 * A format specializes \ref anch::val::CheckContext for its contexts (anch-json: reader and writer contexts, which
 * raise \c anch::json::MappingError ):
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-val/examples/val.cpp context
 *
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-val/examples/val.cpp format
 */
